{"labs": [{"_data": [{"type": "lab_options", "contents": [{"content": true, "type": "has_nav"}, {"content": true, "type": "has_exercise_controls"}]}], "type": "scholar", "stimuli": [{"_data": [], "type": "welcome", "contents": [{}], "template_override": 1, "id": 0}, {"id": 1, "title": "", "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"text": "", "id": 0, "time": "0", "image": "engenn11le02ob01re2img01.jpg"}, {"text": "Many people have experienced a language barrier in another country, but what they don't realize is that others may not understand their gestures, either.\n<br/>\nThere are many examples of cultural differences in gestures that could cause misunderstandings. For instance, in America and Britain, in order to show the number \"one\", a person would hold up his or her index finger. On the other hand, in Switzerland, the thumb is held up. In some cultures, people point at things with a finger, and in others by sticking out their lips or jaw.", "id": 1, "time": "100", "image": "engenn11le02ob01re2img01.jpg"}, {"text": "Sometimes mix-ups can be very embarrassing. Have you ever given the \u201cokay\u201d symbol, for example? It's when you put your thumb and index finger together such that a circle is formed. Some cultures do this so as to indicate that everything's fine and dandy. You may want to avoid doing this overseas, however, as it is quite rude in many countries.", "id": 2, "time": "31200", "image": "engenn11le02ob01re2img02.jpg"}, {"text": "Those who have had to seek medical attention in a foreign country will know that clear communication is particularly important at the hospital. Let's say you've hurt your knee. The attending physician might press the affected area so that he or she can gauge your reaction. A \u201cthumbs-up\u201d might send the wrong message, and an \u201cokay\u201d sign might be seen as an insult. Of course, if you wince or cry out in pain, the doctor should get the picture.<br/>Further complicating the issue is the fact that if you say or do the wrong thing, there might be nothing you can do in order to express your regret. That's because very few cultures have developed a gesture for \u201cI'm sorry.\u201d You could try raising a hand to apologize, but in many places, including North America, you might just end up saying hello!", "id": 3, "time": "52000", "image": "engenn11le02ob01re2img03.jpg"}], "audiosrc": "engenn11le02ob01re2aud01", "coverimg": "engenn11le02ob01re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "_data": [], "type": "audio", "template_override": 1}], "exercises": [{"id": 0, "stimuli_ids": [0], "questions": [], "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"content": "I can talk about health and wellness. ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can talk about what has happened or what I've done.  ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": " I can talk about the purpose of an action or an event.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": " What gesture do you use to say sorry?", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": "Do you know of any cultures with gestures different than your own? ", "type": "Free Header Item"}]}, {"type": "group", "contents": [{"content": "Welcome", "type": "group_name"}]}, {"content": {"correct_feedback": "Correct", "allowed_attempts": 2, "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "You have 2 tries", "type": "text"}, {"content": "you have 1 attempt left", "type": "text"}], "incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "try again", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time", "type": "text"}]}, "type": "feedback"}, {"content": "Ask your partner:", "type": "free_header_text"}, {"content": "Let's find out how to communicate without words!", "type": "free_type_text_area"}], "img_path": "", "type": "WLC", "template_override": 1}, {"id": 1, "stimuli_ids": [1], "questions": [{"_data": [{"type": "question", "contents": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}]}], "responses": [{"id": 0, "_data": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}], "selected": 0, "correct": 0}, {"id": 1, "_data": [{"content":